I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the provided P0/GSAS-II workflow first. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible phase inventory, test candidates against peak positions and intensities, and refine/export the final project and profiles.

The chemistry narrows the first inventory to Pb–W–O phases plus residual PbO/WO₃, but I’ll keep polymorphs and Pb-rich/W-rich intermediates open. I’m now extracting peak families and locating the permitted COD/GSAS-II resources so candidate rejection is based on the whole pattern, not only the strongest lines.

Peak-family matching already supports three coherent components: scheelite PbWO₄ is dominant; monoclinic WO₃ explains the 23–25° triplet and many higher-angle residuals; an orthorhombic PbO candidate explains the strong 29.3° line and a separate sequence near 38.1, 53.4, 56.3, and 63.3°. I’m moving to equal-condition Rietveld screens of these records and their polymorph alternatives.

The first conservative three-phase refinement converged to approximately 77.5 wt% PbWO₄, 10.7 wt% orthorhombic PbO, and 11.8 wt% monoclinic WO₃ (Rwp 16.4% before phase-specific broadening or coordinate work). I’m now challenging each source/polymorph under that identical nuisance model; this is the key inventory check before spending parameters on the fit.

The tetragonal Pb₃O₄ challenge drove its scale effectively to zero and then made its unconstrained cell singular, so that record is rejected rather than interpreted as a real phase. The other alternate-record screens are still converging; I’ll retain only models with positive, stable scales and coherent families.

The monoclinic WO₃ family is confirmed: two physical records both improve the fit and retain ~11.7–11.9 wt%, with COD 2311041 slightly ahead (Rwp 16.22%) of the conventional-cell record 1528915 (16.28%). Their near-equivalence makes source attribution less certain than phase identity; I’m testing the remaining WO₃ polymorphs before choosing the structural parent.

The clean three-phase fit leaves reproducible, narrow residual families at 25.91°, 30.15°, and 31.05° that no selected structure is allowed to absorb through background or width. I’m treating these as a watch-list signal for an additional intermediate/polymorph and will test Pb-rich/W-rich tungstate prototypes as instructed, while also challenging raspite and Pb₃O₄ as explicit fourth phases.

The residual inventory is now chemically and crystallographically coherent: a Pb₂MoO₅ parent (COD 7220560) with the isovalent Mo⁶⁺→W⁶⁺ substitution predicts the previously unexplained lines at 26.00°, 29.41°, 30.75°, 31.19°, 39.55°, 42.03°, 43.52°, 52.17°, and 59.78° (including the common angular correction). Mo⁶⁺ and W⁶⁺ have nearly identical oxide coordination radii, so this is a strong Pb₂WO₅ prototype. I’ll physical-screen it and test whether it replaces the provisional PbO assignment under the same refinement model.

All four prototype tests retained finite positive scales through the scale-only stage; the decisive comparison is now cell/profile convergence. I’m specifically checking whether PbO remains nonzero once Pb₂WO₅ is present, which will distinguish a real precursor residue from a pattern surrogate.

The Pb₂WO₅ model remains at a stable ~15.3 wt% when PbO is restored; PbO also remains stable at ~7.7 wt%. The four-phase inventory improves Rwp from 16.22% to 13.41% under the same deliberately simple common profile, while the Cr-based alternate prototype is decisively worse. I’m now rebuilding the profile from physical values because the unconstrained common-width screen pushed U slightly negative—a baseline warning, not permission to keep that parameter.

A physically bounded common profile prefers U≈0–5, W≈14, but inflates X to ~12.6, confirming that one common width is inadequate. Releasing isotropic size for all phases brings X back to 4.16 and improves Rwp to 12.53%, but the PbO size collapses to 0.008 µm and distorts its fraction—so that all-at-once model is rejected. Isotropic strain is also rejected because three phases refine to negative strain and PbO to an implausible 52,000 ppm. I’m now comparing width terms one phase at a time.

One-at-a-time trials confirm only modest, stable broadening for Pb₂WO₅ (either ~0.31 µm size or ~1,670 ppm strain). PbWO₄ and WO₃ ask for negative strain or no finite size contribution, while the provisional PbO again demands an unphysical width. I’m therefore re-challenging that specific inventory slot—massicot, litharge, Pb₃O₄, and raspite—under the now-established four-phase profile before accepting any fraction.

PbO texture along [100] gives a small, replay-stable gain (March–Dollase 0.88), but it does not explain the excess common width. I’m now enforcing the laboratory’s physical profile range directly: X fixed at 2–6, nonnegative U/W, finite phase sizes, and PbO held at the stated lower size bound rather than allowing the least-squares collapse. This will show whether a bounded model can reproduce isolated low-, mid-, and high-angle widths.

I’m checking the completed bounded-profile trials now. I’ll use the best physically admissible baseline to refine the structural coordinates, then replay the saved project before exporting the final CIFs and profile.

The bounded trials confirm that all four phases retain positive, stable scales. The best admissible common profile has \(R_{wp}\approx13.05\%\); the remaining mismatch is dominated by intensities, so I’m now testing coordinate refinements one phase at a time while holding the accepted width model fixed.

The first unconstrained coordinate tests lowered \(R_{wp}\), but several atoms moved far beyond the generator’s 0.01–0.05 Å mismatch scale, so I’m rejecting those apparent gains as nuisance absorption. I’m returning to the profile model and testing bounded microstrain and instrumental-angle dependence before accepting any atomic displacement.

The bounded profile grid is still refining; these runs are slower because each one replays the full 8,282-point pattern. I’m comparing only replay-stable solutions with nonnegative instrument widths and phase terms inside the stated laboratory ranges.

Microstrain improves the PbO residual only by driving simultaneously to the size and strain bounds, so I’m treating that as a warning rather than an accepted solution. The massicot [100] texture trial is physically bounded and improves several independent reflections; I’m now challenging all phases against the permitted symmetry-distinct texture axes before deciding whether to keep it.

The texture challenge supports [100] March–Dollase terms for Pb₂WO₅ and massicot, both within the stated 0.7–1.4 range and stable on replay; other axes did not give comparable coherent improvement. Polarization refinement is also stable. A two-parameter roughness fit is singular with the background, so I’m pruning it, and the displacement-only geometry trial is unstable.

The residual plot shows coherent broad families near 28.9–32.1°, 46–49°, 54–60°, and 78°, beyond the four-phase profile tails. I’m re-opening the inventory now: testing Pb–W–O intermediates and alternate PbWO₄/WO₃ polymorphs as additions under the accepted common profile, rather than forcing those residuals into extreme PbO broadening or atomic shifts.

The inventory re-check is focused on PbO₂/Pb₃O₄ additions, whose independent line families align most closely with the broad positive residuals. I’ve stopped lower-priority candidates that either generated invalid cells or lacked the key families, so the remaining refinements can converge cleanly.

The rebuilt five-phase model confirms orthorhombic α-PbO₂ at about 4.5 wt%: its scale remains positive, its unique residual families are coherent, and its restrained 0.04 µm profile is replay-stable. I’m now re-challenging the remaining watch list against this new common profile; only additions that retain distinct families and a stable positive scale will survive.

The sixth phase is strongly supported as Pb₃WO₆: its strongest calculated families coincide with the residual groups at 28.88/29.43/30.20°, 44–46°, 53–55°, 59.43°, and 78.05°, and its refined size remains physical. I’m now checking whether the remaining breadth is explainable by allowed microstrain; any term driven beyond 8,000 ppm is being rejected.

With the inventory stabilized at six phases, the remaining unconstrained improvements come from nonphysical coordinate excursions or strain beyond the laboratory bounds. I’m switching to explicitly restrained coordinate updates: each released element is projected back to at most 0.04 Å RMS (0.05 Å for oxygen), then frozen and replayed.

The restrained updates are behaving as intended: PbWO₄ and Pb₂WO₅ each improve the fit after projection to the stated coordinate-mismatch limits, while the unconstrained solutions would have moved much farther and are discarded. I’m applying the same test to the minor oxide phases, where the structural uncertainty is larger.

The restrained structural model now improves without violating the generator’s coordinate-mismatch scale. The Pb₃WO₆ heavy-atom positions are supported; its oxygen update changes \(R_{wp}\) by only ~0.001% and is therefore rejected as indeterminate. I’m performing the same last check on the ~2 wt% Pb₃O₄ phase.

The isolated PbWO₄ families were consistently narrower than the prior calculation. A bounded size grid found a stable interior minimum near 0.5 µm, substantially improving the peak centers without adding strain; I’m refining locally from that physical start now.

The final common-profile grid shows the remaining angle-dependent width trend prefers a high Gaussian \(U\) term; \(U=40\) is the stated laboratory upper bound and improves high-angle peak widths. I’m replaying that bounded endpoint and comparing lower \(X\) values before accepting it, since a boundary solution needs extra scrutiny.

The seven-phase inventory is now coherent, including two W-for-Mo/U prototype substitutions and a weak orthorhombic Pb₃O₄ component. I’m finishing the common-profile challenge now, then I’ll freeze/export the exact replayed project and validate every submitted file against it.

The bounded profile check is internally consistent: keeping \(X=6\) is strongly preferred, and a saved/reopened \(U=40\) model converges reproducibly to Rwp 10.55%. Because both are at the generator’s allowed upper edge, I’m re-challenging the four non-boundary crystallite sizes under that profile before accepting it; this distinguishes genuine broad instrumental peaks from phase-width compensation.

The width re-challenge found a real, replay-stable correction for PbWO₄ (about 0.30 µm) and a smaller but coherent correction for Pb₂WO₅ (about 0.32 µm). WO₃ and Pb₃WO₆ size releases did not improve the replay and are being pruned. I’m freezing the two supported values and doing the final save/reopen cycle now.

The archived project has now passed an independent native GSAS-II replay over all 8,282 original points: the project hash is unchanged, all seven replayed fractions exactly match the export state, and there are no profile-review or geometry/occupancy flags. The replay gives Rwp 10.5067%, Rp 7.2231%, and GOF 3.0075; the submitted CIFs are byte-for-byte identical to CIFs exported independently during verification.

The final full-range visual check confirms the inventory, but it also exposes systematic width/intensity structure in several mid/high-angle families that scalar Rwp alone hides. I’m keeping the verified archive intact while I test phase-specific strain/anisotropic width terms against isolated families; only replay-stable, in-bound terms will replace it.

The phase-width boundary was diagnostic rather than acceptable: the PbO term wanted both minimum size and maximum strain. Re-screening the physical PbO records found the missing explanation—an ordered long-period α-PbO approximant (COD 1533609, Pbcm), whose superlattice families lower Rwp by about 0.61 percentage point at positive scale while passing the contact screen. I’m rebuilding the common profile with this eighth phase and re-testing the now-weaker watch-list components.

The rebuilt eight-phase model now separates genuine residual structure from nuisance compensation. The long-period α-PbO phase remains about 5 wt% after bounded cell/coordinate refinement; α-PbO₂ and orthorhombic Pb₃O₄ remain weak but individually worsen the fit when removed. Axis trials support only two bounded texture corrections: Pb₃WO₆ [100] and long-period α-PbO [010]. I’m freezing the converged cells before the final replay because continued cell release caused a small reflection-list discontinuity despite negligible parameter shifts.

The final accepted model is now stable after cell freezing: eight phases, Rwp 9.0586%, maximum shift/esd 0.017. The new long-period PbO component is structurally distinct from conventional massicot even under broad supercell matching, and all eight exported structures pass the physical screen. I’m performing the last submission-level consistency audit now.

Completed with an eight-phase crystalline model:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| PbWO₄, stolzite | 56.25% |
| Pb₂WO₅ | 13.22% |
| PbO, massicot | 7.25% |
| Monoclinic WO₃ | 8.70% |
| α-PbO₂ | 0.91% |
| Pb₃WO₆ | 7.74% |
| Orthorhombic Pb₃O₄ | 0.76% |
| Long-period α-PbO approximant | 5.17% |

Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Final full-grid statistics are Rwp 9.0586%, Rp 6.4117%, and GOF 2.5906. Native GSAS-II replay reproduced all fractions and profile values without optimization; all CIFs passed physical screening.

